"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkcastor_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkcastor_docs||[]).push([[914],{562(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"introducing-castor","metadata":{"permalink":"/castor-docs/blog/introducing-castor","editUrl":"https://github.com/substratum-labs/castor-docs/edit/main/blog/2026-03-06-introducing-castor.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-06-introducing-castor.md","title":"Introducing Castor: A Secure Microkernel for LLM Agents","description":"LLM agents that autonomously invoke external tools are becoming ubiquitous. But the frameworks that power them \u2014 LangChain, CrewAI, AutoGen \u2014 provide no security boundary between the LLM and the outside world. If an agent can call a tool, it can call it without limit. If it runs too long, it forgets its own instructions. If it hits a dangerous operation, there\'s no systematic way to pause and ask a human.","date":"2026-03-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"announcement","permalink":"/castor-docs/blog/tags/announcement"},{"inline":true,"label":"architecture","permalink":"/castor-docs/blog/tags/architecture"}],"readingTime":3.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Substrate Labs","title":"Castor Team","url":"https://github.com/substratum-labs","imageURL":"https://github.com/substratum-labs.png","key":"substratum-labs","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-castor","title":"Introducing Castor: A Secure Microkernel for LLM Agents","authors":["substratum-labs"],"tags":["announcement","architecture"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"LLM agents that autonomously invoke external tools are becoming ubiquitous. But the frameworks that power them \u2014 LangChain, CrewAI, AutoGen \u2014 provide no security boundary between the LLM and the outside world. If an agent can call a tool, it can call it without limit. If it runs too long, it forgets its own instructions. If it hits a dangerous operation, there\'s no systematic way to pause and ask a human.\\n\\n**Castor is a microkernel that fixes this.** It interposes a deterministic execution engine between the LLM and external tools, providing capability-based budgets, checkpoint/replay, and human-in-the-loop control in a single coherent system.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The Problem\\n\\nWe\'ve been building LLM agents for production use and kept hitting the same three failure modes:\\n\\n**Privilege abuse.** An agent with file access decides to \\"clean up\\" and deletes production data. The framework has no concept of budgets \u2014 if the tool is registered, the agent can call it without restriction.\\n\\n**Resource exhaustion.** An agent asked to \\"research thoroughly\\" makes 3,000 API calls in an hour. There\'s no depletable budget, no cost tracking, no way to set spending limits.\\n\\n**Context amnesia.** After a long conversation, the agent forgets its own safety instructions because they\'ve been pushed out of the context window.\\n\\nThese aren\'t edge cases. They\'re the default behavior of autonomous agents.\\n\\n## The OS Analogy\\n\\nWe noticed something: the relationship between an LLM and external tools is structurally identical to the relationship between a user-space process and the operating system kernel.\\n\\n| OS Concept | Castor Analog |\\n|---|---|\\n| User/kernel space | LLM vs. execution engine |\\n| System calls | Tool invocations via `proxy.syscall()` |\\n| Capabilities | Depletable budget tokens |\\n| Process scheduling | Checkpoint/replay with HITL |\\n| Virtual memory paging | Context window management |\\n\\nThis isn\'t just a metaphor \u2014 it\'s a structural mapping. The trust boundary is real.\\n\\n## What Castor Provides\\n\\n### 1. Capability-Based Security\\n\\nTools declare what they consume. The kernel enforces budgets:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom castor import Castor, castor_tool, SyscallProxy\\n\\n@castor_tool(consumes=\\"api\\", cost_per_use=1.0)\\nasync def web_search(query: str) -> list[str]:\\n    return [f\\"Result for \'{query}\'\\"]\\n\\n@castor_tool(consumes=\\"finance\\", cost_per_use=50.0,\\n             destructive=True, requires_hitl=True)\\nasync def execute_trade(symbol: str, amount: float) -> dict:\\n    ...\\n\\nkernel = Castor(tools=[web_search, execute_trade])\\ncp = await kernel.run(agent, budgets={\\"api\\": 100.0, \\"finance\\": 5000.0})\\n```\\n\\nWhen the budget runs out, the agent gets structured feedback \u2014 not a crash:\\n\\n```\\n\\"Insufficient capability \'api\': need 1.0, have 0.5 remaining.\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe LLM reads this and adjusts its plan.\\n\\n### 2. Checkpoint/Replay\\n\\nYou can\'t pickle Python coroutines. Castor doesn\'t try. Instead, it records every completed tool call in a replay journal. Suspend raises an interrupt; resume replays from the top with cached responses.\\n\\n```\\nRun 1:\\n  web_search(...)    -> executes, logs result\\n  execute_trade(...) -> destructive! SuspendInterrupt raised\\n  Checkpoint saved.\\n\\nHuman approves.\\n\\nRun 2 (replay):\\n  web_search(...)    -> cached (instant)\\n  execute_trade(...) -> HITL approved, execute live\\n  Agent completes.\\n```\\n\\nThis single mechanism gives us HITL, crash recovery, and preemption \u2014 all for free.\\n\\n### 3. Human-in-the-Loop\\n\\nDangerous tools suspend for human review. Humans can:\\n\\n- **Approve** \u2014 execute the tool as requested\\n- **Reject** \u2014 cancel with feedback (\\"Don\'t delete those files\\")\\n- **Modify** \u2014 provide guidance (\\"Only delete files older than 30 days\\")\\n\\nCastor never mutates the agent\'s arguments. On modify, the LLM receives feedback and re-plans. This preserves replay determinism.\\n\\n### 4. Preemptive Scheduling\\n\\n`asyncio.Task.cancel()` injects a `CancelledError` at the next `await` point. Since LLM streaming has an `await` at every chunk boundary, the kernel can preempt an agent at **token-level granularity** \u2014 typically 10-100ms. No shielded sections needed; the fast/slow path split handles double-execution risk.\\n\\n### 5. Context Window Management\\n\\nThe MMU subsystem monitors token usage, pins system prompts (never evicted), and removes stale messages via FIFO when the context window fills up. Agents can retrieve evicted memories with `search_memory`.\\n\\n## Framework Integration\\n\\nCastor works standalone or as a **guard layer** for existing frameworks. We\'ve built proven integrations with:\\n\\n- **smolagents** \u2014 override `execute_tool_call()` to add budget + HITL\\n- **pydantic-ai** \u2014 wrap `WrapperToolset.call_tool()` with budget + HITL\\n\\nBoth integrations add Castor\'s guarantees without changing agent code.\\n\\n## Current Status\\n\\nPhase 1 is complete:\\n- **368 tests**, 0 lint errors\\n- All core requirements implemented (Gate, Scheduler, MMU, Capability)\\n- Sync + async multi-agent spawning with budget delegation\\n- Token-level LLM preemption with proportional budget\\n- `castor.lib` \u2014 agent developer standard library with `tool()`, `chat()`, `react()`, `supervisor()`, and more\\n- Expanded CLI: `castor run`, `castor ps`, `castor inspect`, `castor reject`, `castor modify`\\n\\n## What\'s Next\\n\\n**Phase 2** will push hot paths to Rust via PyO3 for performance. You\'ll still `import castor` in Python \u2014 the Rust core will be a compiled extension module.\\n\\nWe\'re also exploring **Orrery** \u2014 distributed agent orchestration where checkpoints are the portable unit of execution across nodes.\\n\\nRead the [whitepaper](/docs/whitepaper/) for the full technical treatment, or jump to the [quickstart](/docs/getting-started/quickstart) to try it yourself.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*Castor is open source under Apache 2.0. [GitHub](https://github.com/substratum-labs/castor)*"}]}}')}}]);